  "strategy": "<approach_and_rationale>\n    We introduce a novel approach based on cyclic multi-stage optimization, incorporating adjoint sensitivity analysis, multi-scale representation, and dimensionality reduction. This method:\n    1. Combines the strengths of gradient-based optimization and Bayesian search\n    2. Uses a theoretically-grounded decomposition of the optimization problem\n    3. Implements a cycle of: coarse-grid analysis-guided fine-grid optimization\n    4. Focuses on reducing the effective dimension without sacrificing solution quality\n    \n    The core limitation we address is the inability of previous approaches to efficiently escape local optima in high-dimensional spaces while maintaining both feasibility and monotonic improvement. By introducing a cycle-aware interpolation and dimensionality mapping, we enable more effective exploration.\n  </approach_and_rationale>\n  \n  <mathematical_specification>\n    We represent the solution vectorially as h \u2208 \u211d\u207f with:\n    - Integral constraint: \u2211\u1d62 h\u1d62 * dx = 1 (dx = 2/n)\n    - Bounded values: 0 \u2264 h\u1d62 \u2264 1\n    \n    The objective is to minimize:\n      C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)\u00b7(1-h(x+k)) dx\n    \n    We reformulate using discrete convolution:\n      Let C\u2096[0..n-1] = [ (1-h[0..n-1])[::-1] * h[0..n-1] ] with proper modes\n    \n    Key theoretical insight: The cyclic structure allows dimensionality reduction via Hankel matrix eigendecomposition.\n    We decompose:\n      h \u21a6 its autocorrelation representation\n    And optimize in an orthogonal eigenspace to minimize the maximum eigenvalue-penalty.\n  </mathematical_specification>\n  \n  <implementation_procedure>\n    1. Dimension Initialization:\n      - n = 400 (base)\n      - Adaptive grid selection: [400, 800, 1600] incrementally\n    \n    2. Core Algorithm (implemented in run()):\n      - InitializationPhase (2% budget):\n          h = interpolate_to(provided_parent or initial_h_values, new_n)\n          project_feasible(h, target_sum=new_n/2)\n      - ProjectionPhase (10% budget):\n          Build Hankel matrix H using segmented representations\n          Compute leading singular vectors (3 largest)\n          Use these to parameterize solution space (U\u2083D)\n      - OptimizationPhase (80% budget):\n          for each grid size (descending):\n            cycle = AdjointCycleOptimizer(dim=current_d)\n            sol = cycle.optimize_with_adaptive_restart(budget_remaining)\n            discard violated constraints with minimal damage\n          GlobalAdjuster() adjusts across grid sizes\n      - Finalization (8% budget):\n          Re-enforce feasibility with cycle projection\n          recompute_c5_stable(h)\n    \n    3. AdjointOptimizer:\n        Uses stacked incremental projections from projecting the cost function gradient in dual space\n  \n  <search_variations>\n    We implement three dimensionality reduction methods:\n    1. Spectral Reduction: projection onto Hankel matrix eigenspace\n    2. Frame Reduction: using a continuous transformation to set-based representation\n    3. Co-sparse: leveraging sparsity in the frequency domain\n    \n    Each method can be independently dropped or combined with Pareto front selection\n  </implementation_procedure>\n  \n  <compute_budget_and_robustness>\n    Gridwise solvable subproblems:\n      n=400: core complexity dominates time estimate depth=1\n      n=800: \u223c10\u00d7 memory footprint and \u223c25\u00d7 initialization time\n      n=1600: requires 4\u00d7 time and \u223c8\u00d7 memory of n=400\n    \n    We schedule parallel runs with PBS provers based on factorial budget:\n      - Main cycle: 3\u00d7grid with time budget 1300 seconds\n      - Each optimization: 3600 seconds budget per run\n      - Early termination for grid < some threshold\n      - Fallback to previous best solution\n    \n    Memory requirements:\n      n=400: 3.8 GB Python process\n      n=800: 15 GB memory\n      n=1600: >30 GB memory\n  </compute_budget_and_robustness>\n  \n  <validation_and_return_contract>\n    - PrimaryOutput: \n        (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n    - Each returned vector h_values must satisfy:\n        Integral(h) = 1\n        For all i: 0 \u2264 h_values[i] \u2264 1\n    - Validation steps:\n        Compute using fallback compute_c5_stable() (method used in parent)\n        Check against parent's evaluation for close values (tol=1e-8)\n        Ensure serialized representation can be re-minimized\n    \n    Fallback behavior:\n        If any operation fails, use the projected feasible solution from parent\n  </validation_and_return_contract>",
